<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uruguayan Civil War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a>–<a href="/years/1851">1851</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Guerra Grande · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Uruguayan Civil War, known as the Guerra Grande, was fought from 1839 to 1851 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorados</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fructuoso-rivera">Fructuoso Rivera</a> and the Blancos of Manuel Oribe. Oribe was backed by the Argentine ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-manuel-de-rosas">Juan Manuel de Rosas</a>, while the Colorados had support at times from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Italian volunteers under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> withstood a siege of nearly nine years, and the war ended in 1851 with no victor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uruguayan-civil-war.jpg" alt="Uruguayan Civil War" width="320" height="291" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: KaasterMDK · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guerra_Grande.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The conflict grew out of rivalries among the leaders of Uruguayan independence. After the proclamation of independence in 1828, Juan Lavalleja, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-three-orientals">Thirty-Three Orientals</a>, and the veteran commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fructuoso-rivera">Fructuoso Rivera</a> contended for primacy. Rivera was chosen the first President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> on 6 November 1830 and defeated Lavalleja at Tupambaé on 18 September 1832. Lavalleja fled to the Brazilian province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande-do-sul">Rio Grande do Sul</a>, and an invasion he launched in March 1834 with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-manuel-de-rosas">Juan Manuel de Rosas</a> also failed.</p><p>Manuel Oribe, another of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-three-orientals">Thirty-Three Orientals</a>, was elected the second President on 1 March 1835. In January 1836 he removed Rivera from the army command, and on 16 July 1836 Rivera rebelled. Oribe's soldiers wore a white armband and Rivera's first wore blue, then red, since blue faded. From these marks came the conservative Blancos and the liberal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorados</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Rivera was beaten at Carpintería on 19 September 1836 but, with help from the Riograndense Republic, defeated Oribe at Yucutujá in 1837 and at Palmar on 15 June 1838. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, then blockading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, backed Rivera, and Oribe resigned on 24 October 1838 and fled to Buenos Aires. Rosas refused to recognise Rivera and sought Oribe's return, and British and French intervention turned the struggle into an international war. On 6 December 1842 Oribe's Blancos crushed Rivera's army at Arroyo Grande and laid siege to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a>.</p><p>The siege lasted nine years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> had about thirty thousand inhabitants in 1843, only a third of them Uruguayan, and its defence rested largely on newly freed slaves, who numbered 5,000, and on foreign exiles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> had two governments: the Gobierno de la Defensa in Montevideo under Joaquín Suárez and the Gobierno del Cerrito under Oribe. British and French fleets protected the city from the sea, and French, Spanish and Italian legionnaires under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a> fought for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorados</a>. Britain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> withdrew in 1850 after a treaty favourable to Rosas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>A rising against Rosas by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justo-jose-de-urquiza">Justo José de Urquiza</a>, governor of Entre Ríos, changed the situation in 1851. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> intervened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> in May 1851, and Rosas fell at Caseros in February 1852, after which Urquiza's forces lifted the siege. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a> then signed five treaties with Brazil, providing for perpetual alliance, joint navigation of the Río Uruguay, and the return of runaway slaves. Uruguay renounced its claims north of the Río Cuareim. Both sides had abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> during the war, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> industry was devastated.</p><p>Party feuding did not end. In 1870 the parties agreed spheres of influence, and the Revolution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lance">Lances</a> followed in 1870 to 1872. The French author <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-dumas">Alexandre Dumas</a> described the siege in his novel The New Troy as a new Trojan war.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uruguayan Civil War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The new country had just under 75,000 people and two parties, the Blancos of the second president, Manuel Oribe and the Colorados of the first, Fructuoso Rivera, whose quarrel became the <b>Guerra Grande</b> in 1839.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oriental-republic-of-uruguay">Oriental Republic of Uruguay · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
